## Intuition
In Pascal’s Triangle, each element in a row follows the combination formula nCr.

Instead of calculating factorials (which are slow and can overflow), we can use a mathematical relationship:

This allows us to compute each element using the previous one.

So instead of building the whole triangle, we generate only the required row efficiently.

## Approach
1) The first element is always 1.

2) Each next element can be calculated using:

   curr = prev * (n - i) / i

3) We keep updating the previous value and store the result in an array.

4) This avoids factorial calculation.

5) This works in linear time.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
  public int[] pascalTriangleII(int r) {
    int[] ans = new int[r];
    int curr = 1;
    int prev = 1;
    ans[0] = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i < r; i++) {
      prev = ans[i - 1];
      curr = prev * (r - i) / i;
      ans[i] = curr;
    }
    return ans;
  }
}

```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:** 
O(r) – We compute each element once.
**Space Complexity:**
O(r) – We store the row in an array.7c:T4b

